			<div class="content" id="post-content-4864765">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Does anyone know why this is said,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because readers don't find the material interesting? If something doesn't interest them on the mainsite, they're not obligated to explain exactly why the piece didn't spark their interest. If someone is expecting thorough critique from a reviewer, the forums and IRC chatrooms are the place to look.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>because I am curious as to whether this helpful to the author</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While it's not the most helpful feedback, it's more helpful than nothing at all. Disliking something for not being interesting enough is different from disliking something for being too long-winded and a slog to read, or disliking something because it uses a color scheme or unique format that is disorienting or difficult to follow.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>as one person could find it disinterestong but the next could find it absolutely fascinating.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Just because some people find something interesting does not mean everyone else has to feel the same way, and vice versa. People can disagree with others about the kind of article they like to read. Sometimes an author has to just shrug and accept a downvote. Just because someone else likes something does not make it wrong to dislike the same thing for personal preference reasons.</p>

							</div>

								
					
